Here’s a beginner-friendly guide based on a YouTube demonstration of CAT6 RJ45 crimping. The video shows stripping, arranging the wires in T568B order, inserting them into the RJ45 plug, crimping, and testing. ([YouTube][1])

[How to Make a Cat 6 Ethernet Cable | RJ45 T568B Wiring & Crimping Tutorial](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ujv7trbDJxU&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

 Step-by-step: Attach an RJ45 connector to CAT6 cable

1. Prepare the tools

   * CAT6 Ethernet cable
   * RJ45 connector
   * RJ45 crimping tool
   * Cable stripper/cutter
   * LAN cable tester

2. Cut the cable

   * Cut the CAT6 cable to the required length.
   * Make a clean, straight cut.

3. Strip the outer jacket

   * Remove about 2–3 cm of the outer plastic jacket.
   * Be careful not to damage the small wires inside.
   * Remove the CAT6 separator/spline if present. ([PSSCIVE][2])

4. Separate and straighten the wires

   * Carefully separate the four twisted pairs.
   * Untwist only as much as necessary.
   * Straighten all 8 wires with your fingers.

5. Arrange the wires using T568B

   Looking at the RJ45 plug with the contacts facing you, arrange the wires from pin 1 to pin 8 as:

   ```text
   Pin   Wire Color
   ---   ----------------
    1    White/Orange
    2    Orange
    3    White/Green
    4    Blue
    5    White/Blue
    6    Green
    7    White/Brown
    8    Brown
   ```

   This is the T568B sequence shown in the tutorial. ([YouTube][1])

6. Trim the wires

   * Hold all 8 wires tightly together in the correct order.
   * Cut the ends straight and evenly.
   * Keep the exposed untwisted section as short as practical for CAT6 performance. ([WiFi Speed][3])

7. Insert the wires into the RJ45 connector

   * Hold the wires in their correct order.
   * Push all 8 wires completely into the RJ45 plug.
   * Check through the transparent connector that every wire reaches the front.
   * Make sure some of the outer cable jacket enters the connector for proper strain relief. ([DLAY Template Project][4])

8. Crimp the connector

   * Place the RJ45 plug into the correct slot of the crimping tool.
   * Squeeze the handles firmly until the crimp is complete.
   * The metal contacts should press into the individual wires and secure the cable jacket. ([WiFi Speed][3])

9. Inspect the connection

   * Check that the wire colors are still in the correct order.
   * Make sure all 8 wires are fully seated.
   * Check that the connector is firmly attached to the cable.

10. Test the cable

* Connect both ends to a LAN cable tester.
* A correctly terminated straight-through cable should show the eight connections in the expected 1-to-8 sequence.
* If a pin is missing or appears in the wrong position, cut off the connector and terminate it again. ([surveilforge.com][5])

